// ⭐⭐ **`__Builtins.slabRemoteFreeCount()` — THE ALLOCATOR'S ONE OUTCOME QUERY, and the only way a program
// can observe that a box was freed on a machine other than the one that allocated it.** It answers the sum of
// every processor's remote-free counter, which is 0 on the one-processor default and rises the moment a
// service running on one machine drops a payload built on another.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE CROSS-PROCESSOR FREE ROAD WAS EXERCISED AND NOT OBSERVED.**
// `multicore-stress/service-torture` and `multicore-stress/service-fanin-torture` push thousands of heap boxes across machines in
// each direction, and nothing anywhere read a number that would move if the road stopped being taken — a
// regression routing every free down the LOCAL arm would have left both tortures and the whole suite green.
// `specs/slab-sharding.md`'s `ok=yes` is what this makes an assertion rather than a hope.
//
// ⚠ The name is invented here, on the argumentless-integer shape the family already has: nothing
// else exposes this counter as a language surface. `SlabRuntime.SlabRemoteFreeCountName` owns what
// it costs; this file only routes the name.
let BuiltinsSlabRemoteFreeCountName = b"__Builtins.slabRemoteFreeCount"

// It takes NOTHING. Its own constant, for `CurrentProcessIdArity`'s rule.
let SlabRemoteFreeCountArity = 0

